WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.710
- Could you imagine being on the bus

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.510
and somebody whips out a can of air?

00:00:06.392 --> 00:00:09.975
(jazzy instrumental music)

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.230
- So since here at Buzzfeed
we are out of ideas,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.070
today we're trying expensive air.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.450
- I honestly think it's the craziest thing

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.870
I have ever heard in my life.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.720
- If this air is really expensive,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.780
I do expect it to give
me all the oxygen intake

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.530
that I need for the month and also maybe

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.680
if it gets me a little
high that'd be cool.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.450
- I'm very picky about what I breathe

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:32.930
so maybe I'll pay for it.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.723
It depends on how good it is.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.530
- Edie, you got that kinda money?

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.250
This is like drugs.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.360
- It's so light.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.277
- It's so light, I'm like,
"There's nothing in here."

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:53.063
- Why?

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.493
- Instructions, open cap and enjoy.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.373
- What?

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.233
- Oh.

00:01:01.749 --> 00:01:03.499
Ah, I figured it out!

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.205
- Alright, guys, we're
gonna sniff some air.

00:01:09.205 --> 00:01:11.872
(cans spraying)

00:01:14.426 --> 00:01:16.166
What the heck is this?

00:01:16.166 --> 00:01:16.999
- Ah.

00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:19.233
- It was literally like air.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.223
- I don't taste anything,
I don't feel anything.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.670
- It's flip-flopping air,
it's air, this is air.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.550
This is normal air, it
tastes like the same air

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:30.150
that we are breathing right now.

00:01:39.663 --> 00:01:43.340
- I think these facts are
about to be, like, depressing.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.960
- According to the World
Health Organization,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.760
air pollution poses the greatest

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.910
environmental risk to human health.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:53.470
And in 2016, the majority of outdoor

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:56.623
air pollution-related
deaths occurred in Asia.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:58.203
Wow.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.900
- I used to live in Korea and
I remember during soccer games

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:06.260
we had to test the yellow
dust that was outside

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.330
because if it was too
much we couldn't play.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:09.503
- That's really sad.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:10.770
Air pollution is considered the

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.540
fourth-largest threat to human health,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.353
following blood pressure,
dietary risks, and smoking.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.123
- Wow.

00:02:17.123 --> 00:02:21.140
- I think it's even worse
because smoking is by choice.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.420
You can choose not to
but air, what do you do?

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:25.253
Stay home?

00:02:25.253 --> 00:02:27.610
- Yeah, and even then the
air comes in from outside.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.220
- Some scientists even believe that

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.203
air pollution causes birth defects.

00:02:36.186 --> 00:02:37.019
- Damn.

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:40.710
- So in 2016, 2.6 million deaths were

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.643
linked to air pollution in the house,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.823
and 4.3 million deaths were
linked to outdoor air pollution.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.453
That's six million people in one year?

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:50.733
Dang.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.700
Now I'm really sad and I
shouldn't be wasting this air.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.110
- I feel like there's no fixing it.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:57.943
We done (beep) up.

00:02:57.943 --> 00:02:59.210
Just don't have kids, everyone.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.560
- I grew up in China until I was 23.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.280
By the time I was 18, like,
the air in Beijing just,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.030
like, you can't really see each other

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.180
when you, like, walk towards people.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.380
I feel fine so far but who know if there's

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.493
something it's done to me, you know?

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.243
- I understand why there's canned air.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.910
Looking at these facts, pollution is

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:21.050
a big problem that we have.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:24.340
- It sounds silly to pay
for air but if you're

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.520
in an environment where
you literally can't breathe

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.420
or there's something wrong
with your air or air pollution,

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.083
this is a temporary fix to your problem.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.150
- Moral of the story,
treat our planet better

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.233
so we don't need canned air.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.380
- This is a great product for
people that really need it.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.720
It should really open up
conversations about air pollution

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.590
and what we can do to make and create

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:49.740
a better environment
for all people to live

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:52.333
because seven million people dying a year,

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.913
that's not good so let's
figure it out together.

00:03:59.016 --> 00:04:01.683
(cans spraying)

